comments.body:
**Please check cut-v5d instead of cut-v5b**, if you haven't finished (release cut-v5d, `film.mp4` at the commit just pushed). It differs from cut-v5b only in:
- mix-cut-v5c: strike 4 starts at 80.34 so its ignition peaks at 80.46–80.50, on the flare;
- s18 1:49.5–1:53.8 uses dp's revised frame, with her against the left wall as in s17;
- the s13a memory (1:12–1:20) is one continuous pull-back instead of two framings;
- two-sentence subtitles are split at their pause (30 cues; e.g. "Her hands were numb." 32.43–33.92, "If she dared strike just one…" from 34.42).

Every other shot, line start and effect is identical. `edit/cut-v5d/verify.txt`: no must-fails. If your cut-v5b findings are already written, they carry over except at these four places.

**Correction to the cut-v5 event times** (EDL 0072b12). dp's frame sequences (shots-b2) replace the single stills, so a few events moved:
- goose vision goes dark **56.9–57.4 s**, not 56.1–56.8;
- bundle: first flare dissolves in **90.83–91.18 s**, full blaze **91.93–92.28 s**;
- s12 runs: match out/tree dims 64.0, lights lift from 65.8, stars over the roofs from 67.5, **star falls from 69.0–69.4** ("One fell");
- 1:49–1:59 is now one shot: the townspeople dissolve up into the dawn sky at 113.26–113.76, with the fade to black 117.96–118.96.

Unchanged from my last message: stove dark 44.24–45.44, s09 fades up at 46.333, 4th-strike flare at 80.50.

Also from the director (editor#9): a new take of "Someone is dying" (70.32) is on its way from you. I'll lay it in with the mix that carries it.

issue.body:
**Need:** a screening of `film.mp4` in director release **`screen-cut-v5d`** (121.96 s, burned-in English subtitles). It's the cut I intend to lock. Watch it as a first-time viewer and write timecoded notes as before.

**For:** the lock. I lock only a cut you have watched. Since screen-cut-v5b, the changes are small:
- the memory of her grandmother (about 1:12–1:20) is one continuous move;
- her place in the last two shots matches;
- the fourth match's sound sits on its flare;
- each sentence's subtitle leaves with it.

Please say whether anything stops a first-time viewer from following or feeling the film, and whether anything is new or worse than in screen-cut-v5b.

**Waiting on this:** the lock.
